<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Tarnobrzeg</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1919 CE · capital Tarnobrzeg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived peasant republic in south-eastern Poland, led by a radical priest who demanded land reform after the First World War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In November 1918, as Austrian authority in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a> collapsed, a radical priest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugeniusz-okon">Eugeniusz Okon</a>, and the peasant activist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomasz-dabal">Tomasz Dabal</a> called on peasants and returning soldiers around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnobrzeg">Tarnobrzeg</a> to take power. At a rally on 6 November they proclaimed a republic in the district.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The movement demanded the division of large estates among peasants and the removal of landlords and officials. Its leaders formed a local council and militia, and peasants seized estate grain and forests. It never had a settled administration or defined borders and was more a social revolt than a state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The new Polish government sent troops to restore order, and by spring 1919 the movement had been suppressed and its leaders arrested. Both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugeniusz-okon">Okon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomasz-dabal">Dabal</a> were later elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a>, and Dabal became a communist.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-tarnobrzeg.jpg" alt="Republic of Tarnobrzeg" width="320" height="311"><figcaption>Image: Nikodem62 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Republika-Tarnobrzeska.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tarnobrzeg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eugeniusz Okon, Tomasz Dabal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tarnobrzeg Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=republic-of-tarnobrzeg" aria-label="Republic of Tarnobrzeg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5174 421 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Tarnobrzeg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5494 677L5473 630"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5473 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5494 677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5494 677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tarnobrzeg. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=republic-of-tarnobrzeg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

